/* Wealth Lab · Module 2 Academic course sidecar · 2026-11-01 · append-only */
window.WealthLabAcademicM2Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m2-academic-v1","date":"2026-11-01","lessonIds":["L2.S1","L2.S2","L2.S3","L2.S4","L2.S5"],"lessons":[{"id":"L2.S1","title":"What a Mortgage Really Is","subtitle":"Property rights, secured credit, underwriting and household solvency","prerequisites":["L1.S1 household balance sheet","L1.S2 dated cash-flow calendar","L1.S3 discounting","L1.S4 fixed-payment debt"],"objectives":["Construct buyer and lender closing balance sheets without double-counting equity","Derive the level-payment formula from discounted cash flows","Calculate LTV, DTI, household cash margin and stress exposures","Identify the instruments and parties that make a mortgage enforceable","Evaluate a purchase with an unfavorable appraisal and uncertain repair costs"],"sections":[{"title":"The home, the debt, and the legal claim are three different objects","body":["A mortgage transaction brings together a home purchase and a financing contract. The borrower acquires an ownership interest in real property; the lender supplies money that the borrower promises to repay; a recorded security instrument creates a claim against property if that promise is not fulfilled. The note, mortgage or deed of trust, deed, title search, and insurance contracts have different purposes. They should never be described as interchangeable documents. State law controls how the security instrument operates and how foreclosure proceeds.","The buyer's asset is the property at a supportable economic value, not the original purchase price forever. The buyer's liability is the loan payoff balance plus any other enforceable obligations. Home equity is the difference between the asset value and secured claims, but is not an extra asset to add on top of the property value. In a household balance sheet record the house and loan separately; this is also how professionals detect falling collateral coverage.","Suppose a Connecticut household pays $250,000 for a home using $12,500 cash down and $237,500 of first-mortgage financing. At closing the house enters the balance sheet at an assumed $250,000 value and the loan enters at $237,500. The equity is $12,500. If the buyer paid an additional $7,500 in nonrecoverable transaction costs, that expenditure reduces cash and net worth; the down payment itself merely shifts wealth from cash into the property. This distinction avoids the false claim that getting a mortgage immediately creates wealth.","Mortgage lenders care about the borrower's ability to pay and collateral value because the contract is a stream of uncertain future payments secured by property. The lender may fund a loan through deposits, warehouse credit, or secondary-market financing; a servicer may later collect the payments on someone else's behalf. Selling or transferring the loan does not automatically alter the signed note terms. The economics of credit pricing reflect funding, default risk, expected prepayment, servicing, capital, and market conditions."],"worked":["Purchase price $250,000; down payment $12,500; new secured loan $237,500.","Before closing: cash $30,000, no other assets or liabilities in this isolated illustration; net worth $30,000.","Closing cash: $12,500 down + $7,500 unrecoverable fees = $20,000; remaining cash $10,000.","After closing: property $250,000 + cash $10,000 − mortgage $237,500 = $22,500 net worth.","Thus transaction costs, not the $12,500 down payment, caused the modeled $7,500 decline in net worth."],"practice":[["Why does recording home equity as a separate asset double-count wealth?","Because the home asset minus the mortgage already determines equity; adding it separately counts the same ownership interest twice."],["If the property loses 8% immediately and the debt is unchanged, calculate equity.","Property value $230,000; equity −$7,500, before selling costs."]]},{"title":"Discounting mortgage payments: why amortization has this formula","body":["A fixed-rate fully amortizing mortgage promises equal scheduled principal-and-interest payments at regular intervals, with the last payment retiring the debt. The loan amount today must equal the present value of those promised payments discounted at the contractual periodic rate. This is the same time-value-of-money idea introduced in Module 1, now applied from the lender's perspective. The rate per month is the contractual annual nominal rate divided by twelve if the note uses that monthly convention; actual daily-interest and first-payment details can differ.","Let principal P be advanced at time zero, r be the periodic interest rate, n the number of monthly payments, and A the fixed payment. The discounted-value identity is P=A/(1+r)+A/(1+r)^2+...+A/(1+r)^n. Multiply by (1+r) and subtract the original series. Cancellation yields P r=A[1−(1+r)^(-n)]. Rearrange to obtain A=P r/[1−(1+r)^(-n)]. At r=0, take the limiting value A=P/n rather than divide by zero.","The interest portion of month k is the opening balance multiplied by r; principal repaid is the installment minus interest. The outstanding balance follows B_k=B_(k−1)(1+r)−A. An equivalent closed form is B_k=P(1+r)^k−A[((1+r)^k−1)/r]. These identities give two independent ways to validate a spreadsheet. If either schedule does not reconcile to the note's payment and maturity, inspect rate units, payment dates, rounding and fees.","A fixed monthly payment does not imply fixed interest dollars. As principal declines, each future month's interest falls and more of the same payment goes toward principal. Negative amortization describes the opposite possibility: unpaid interest is added to the balance under certain product structures. It is not a feature of the assumed conventional fully amortizing schedule. Mortgage insurance, property taxes and hazard insurance sit outside the P&I equation, even when paid in the same monthly transfer."],"worked":["Assume loan P=$237,500; note rate 6.50% annually; monthly r=0.065/12; term n=360.","Payment A=$237,500×(0.065/12)/[1−(1+0.065/12)^(-360)] = approximately $1,501.16.","Month-one interest: $237,500×0.065/12=$1,286.46; month-one principal ≈ $214.70.","After 12 payments B_12≈$234,845.40; principal reduced ≈$2,654.60.","After 60 payments B_60≈$222,326.07. The unpaid balance remains substantial despite $90,069.60 of cumulative scheduled P&I."],"practice":[["What does the P&I payment omit?","Property tax, homeowners insurance, mortgage insurance, HOA or association assessments, utilities, repairs and possible separately charged fees."],["Why is first-month principal small?","Interest is charged on nearly the full opening balance; with a long term and positive rate, much of the early level payment covers interest."]]},{"title":"Underwriting is not the same as household affordability","body":["Underwriting evaluates whether an applicant satisfies the lender and product's documented risk constraints. Common inputs include credit history, verifiable income, employment continuity, existing obligations, assets, property characteristics, appraisal, and collateral title. Approval does not prove that a mortgage is comfortable for an individual's real household. A lender may exclude costs that are economically unavoidable, or assess gross income while the family's bills are paid from net pay.","Loan-to-value (LTV) generally compares financing to an eligible collateral value; for a purchase, underwriting may depend on the lower of purchase price and appraised value under program rules. Combined loan-to-value (CLTV) adds eligible subordinate liens in the numerator. Debt-to-income (DTI) compares defined recurring monthly debt obligations to qualifying gross monthly income. It is not the percentage of take-home pay that remains after groceries, medical care, commuting, and irregular household needs.","Consider a $250,000 agreed price with an appraisal of $240,000. A lender using the lesser figure to enforce a 95% maximum first-lien LTV would cap the mortgage at $228,000, not the $237,500 originally planned. The transaction then needs another $9,500 of buyer cash, seller price reduction, or a different eligible financing arrangement. A lower appraisal does not itself imply the home must be bought at the agreed price; the purchase contract and financing contingencies matter.","Use both a lender-compliance worksheet and a household survival worksheet. The first shows verified gross earnings and underwriting debts. The second lists actual cash after taxes, utilities, food, transportation, disability coverage, childcare, periodic replacements, home maintenance and reserves. If the home can be approved but the latter budget has no shock capacity, the prudent recommendation may be to buy less, save more, modify debts or delay."],"worked":["Hypothetical gross monthly income $6,000; car/other lender-counted debt $450; proposed housing payment including taxes, insurance and mortgage insurance $2,050.","Back-end DTI=($2,050+$450)/$6,000=41.67%; no universal lender approval rule follows.","Cash take-home $4,550; housing $2,050; $450 existing debt; $1,600 essential nondebt spending; $300 repair reserve leaves $150.","On a $250,000 property, $237,500 loan is 95% nominal price LTV; against $240,000 appraisal the same loan is 98.96%.","At a hypothetical 95% collateral LTV cap, permitted first loan $228,000; additional purchase-cash shortfall $9,500."],"practice":[["Calculate DTI if $700 extra debt is added to the $2,500 monthly obligations.","($2,500+$700)/$6,000 = 53.33%; discuss underwriting and cash-flow consequences."],["Why might an applicant pass DTI but fail personal stress testing?","Gross income and a lender's defined obligations do not capture all taxes, necessities, unexpected home repairs, job changes or goals."]]},{"title":"Rates, fees, incentives, and risk ownership","body":["The mortgage note rate is not a full price comparison. Fees, discount points, third-party charges, lender credits and timing of cash flows change economic borrowing cost. Annual percentage rate (APR) is a regulated disclosure intended to include specified finance charges; it is generally different from the advertised note rate. An APR cannot, by itself, forecast refinance timing, property taxes, or future adjustable-rate changes. Compare contracts with the same amount, term and assumptions rather than isolate one headline number.","A discount point usually costs one percent of the principal per point and can buy a lower contractual rate. The exchange is uncertain because the homeowner may sell or refinance before savings repay the upfront cost. In the simplified comparison of a $237,500 30-year loan at 6.50% versus 6.25%, the lower-rate payment is approximately $38.83 per month lower. Paying $2,375 for that rate implies a naive cash-payment break-even around 61.2 months; an accurate comparison also considers differing remaining balances and the time value of the upfront fee.","Borrowers face payment and income risk; lenders or investors face default and prepayment risk; title insurers insure specified title defects rather than promising property values will not fall. Mortgage insurance generally protects the lender or guarantor against some credit losses, not the borrower's equity. Distinguish who receives a payment, what claim it covers, and whether it makes the household more resilient.","A refinance is not free money. It extinguishes or modifies old borrowing through a new transaction subject to prevailing prices, fees, underwriting, and possibly an appraisal. A shorter remaining term on the old loan makes a nominally lower new 30-year monthly payment especially deceptive: the borrower may pay for longer. Financial professionals model cash interest and fees, payoff balances, the repayment horizon and alternative uses of cash; they also keep the decision reversible if refinancing is only theoretical."],"worked":["Illustrative principal $237,500, 360 months. Offer A 6.50%: payment ≈$1,501.16.","Offer B 6.25%: payment ≈$1,462.33; upfront point cost $2,375.","Monthly P&I saving ≈$38.83; simple break-even = $2,375/$38.83 ≈61.2 months.","After 60 installments, unpaid balance A ≈$222,326.07, B ≈$221,675.95, a $650.12 difference in principal wealth.","A complete 5-year comparison incorporates payments, points, payoff balance difference, discounting and taxes under declared assumptions."],"practice":[["Would one point universally reduce the note rate by 0.25 percentage points?","No. The exchange rate between points and note rate depends on the lender, product and market; the example assumes a specific negotiated offer."],["Why is a five-year horizon informative even for a 30-year mortgage?","A sale or refinance may stop the original payment stream well before contractual maturity; compare the expected holding period and downside horizons."]]},{"title":"Closing evidence, servicing, and default","body":["The federally regulated Loan Estimate normally arrives early in covered transactions, and the Closing Disclosure supplies final loan terms near settlement. Under the CFPB's general TRID framework, qualifying application information triggers a Loan Estimate deadline measured in three business days, while the Closing Disclosure must be received at least three business days before consummation. Some products, including HELOCs and reverse mortgages, use different disclosure forms. A student must identify the loan type before applying the rule mechanically.","A mortgage package is evidence of a sequence, not simply paperwork to sign. Check the buyers' names, vesting of title, location and legal description, interest-rate lock, type of loan, whether principal or payment can change, points, escrows, lender credits, cash to close, closing attorney and lender instructions. Read both promissory note and security instrument. A recorded deed controls the stated ownership interest; a beneficiary designation on another asset does not necessarily change real-property title.","A loan servicing transfer means the recipient of payments may change while the legal note terms remain. The borrower should verify transfer notices and payment instructions against a trusted channel to avoid wire and payment redirection scams. Keep annual escrow and tax statements, insurance records, correspondence and payment history. If a rate adjusts or escrow changes, the total amount due can shift even if principal amortization was modeled correctly at origination.","Missed payments lead to remedies specified by contract, federal servicing rules and state law, potentially including foreclosure, deficiency questions, credit consequences and loss of housing. A secured loan creates meaningful downside asymmetry: a household can lose its down payment and have additional obligations. Professional planning therefore keeps sufficient emergency liquidity and evaluates insurance, disability, job stability and legal support before borrowing at the upper end of any modeled limit."],"worked":["On a hypothetical Loan Estimate: interest rate 6.50%, APR 6.78%, 30-year fixed, total estimated cash to close $19,500.","Do not substitute 6.78% into the amortization calculation: the scheduled principal-and-interest payment uses the note-rate conventions.","Compare the Closing Disclosure line by line: note rate, loan amount, loan terms, points, lender credits, third-party fees, escrow, and cash to close.","If escrow expenses rise $1,200 per year, required monthly funding may increase by about $100 before shortage repayment, subject to the actual annual analysis.","Record documentary mismatches and request corrections before signing instead of relying on verbal assurances."],"practice":[["What is the difference between an APR and a note rate?","The note rate sets scheduled contractual interest under the note; APR includes specified upfront finance charges for comparison and can differ materially."],["Why preserve the original Loan Estimate after closing?","It provides evidence for comparing disclosed terms and categories of charges against the final Closing Disclosure."]]},{"title":"Independent mortgage risk memo","body":["A professional recommendation is a conditional statement supported by measured evidence. The analyst starts with the household's economic objective: stable shelter, sufficient cash, appropriate title, and an affordable long-run payment. Next the analyst separately estimates income, unavoidable spending, reserves, valuation, financing, closing costs, property-condition risk and alternative housing choices. A single maximum qualifying loan is not a recommendation.","Build a base case, a cash shock and an asset-value shock. For the $250,000 property and $237,500 initial mortgage, imagine an 8% value decline soon after closing. The property is worth $230,000 and the household has −$7,500 home equity before selling costs. A 6% hypothetical selling cost would absorb another $13,800, making estimated gross-sale proceeds after debt −$21,300 if the loan balance had not been reduced. Negative proceeds mean the seller would need additional funds, negotiation or other resolution; the model does not predict legal remedies.","Now test income, not just equity. If housing costs are $2,050, existing debt payments $450, essential nondebt spending $1,600 and repairs reserve $300, the family needs $4,400 of monthly cash for these allocations. Net income of $4,550 leaves a $150 buffer, but a one-month loss of 25% of take-home pay produces a $987.50 deficit for that month. This is why the lender's DTI ratio and cash reserves cannot be examined independently.","The final memo should state what is known, what is an assumption, what must be verified in writing and which options remain possible. Score proposals on affordability and resilience, not the excitement of owning an asset. Compare purchase now, buy a lower-cost home, rent longer while growing reserves, or restructure an existing expensive debt. State which missing evidence could reverse the recommendation and who should verify it: lender, licensed inspector, attorney, insurer or local assessor."],"worked":["Asset shock: $250,000 × 0.92 = $230,000 estimated property value.","Equity: $230,000 − $237,500 = −$7,500 before selling expense.","6% sale expense: $230,000 × 0.06 = $13,800; proceeds net of mortgage and modeled sale costs = −$21,300.","Household cash margin: $4,550 − $2,050 − $450 − $1,600 − $300 = $150/month.","At 75% of normal take-home, monthly income $3,412.50; with $4,400 obligations and allocations, deficit $987.50."],"practice":[["Draft one paragraph defending the decision to delay even when lender underwriting approves.","A valid answer quantifies a thin cash margin, credible stress and the benefit of growing unrestricted reserves; distinguish the lender's requirements from personal affordability."],["What evidence could justify purchasing now instead?","Verified durable income, reliable reserves after closing, lower house expense, repair report, realistic insurance and tax estimates, and contractually verified loan terms."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CFPB · Mortgage Loan Estimate","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"],["CFPB · Closing Disclosure and timing","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/close/review-documents-before-closing/"],["CFPB · DTI","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-debt-to-income-ratio-en-1791/"],["CFPB · LTV","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-loan-to-value-ratio-and-how-does-it-relate-to-my-costs-en-121/"],["CFPB · Points and lender credits","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/how-should-i-use-lender-credits-and-points-also-called-discount-points-en-136/"]],"assessment":{"title":"Loan analysis memo","prompt":"Prepare a one-page recommendation for the buyer using the $250,000 case. Supply the at-closing balance sheet, one-year amortization check, collateral stress scenario, household DTI versus cash-budget comparison and three annotated source citations. Explicitly defend a buy, renegotiate or delay recommendation.","rubric":["Reconciled closing balance sheet (20%)","Correct payment, interest and loan-balance calculations (20%)","Distinct LTV, DTI and cash-flow analyses (20%)","Quantified adverse scenario and plausible alternatives (20%)","Source quality, assumptions and professional judgment (20%)"]}},{"id":"L2.S2","title":"Down Payment vs. Reserves","subtitle":"Liquidity, closing-cost funding, opportunity cost and resilience","prerequisites":["L1.S1 liquidity and net worth","L1.S2 cash forecasts","L2.S1 loan and title distinctions"],"objectives":["Reconcile cash-to-close accounting without double-counting deposits and credits","Estimate mortgage insurance and LTV tradeoffs without claiming universal pricing","Construct an unrestricted post-closing emergency reserve plan","Compare assistance loans with personal savings and model early-exit exposure","Stress-test the purchase against an appraisal gap and repair bills"],"sections":[{"title":"An upfront payment is not the complete purchase budget","body":["A homebuyer's down payment is the portion of the price not supplied by purchase financing; it is not the same as total cash needed to complete a purchase. Closing costs comprise distinct lender charges, settlement services, government and title charges, prepaid items, and initial escrow deposits. A useful budget distinguishes nonrecoverable costs, amounts paid in advance for future services, and funds that remain assets such as refundable escrow balances. The simplified examples below treat all stated fees as consumed, but a rigorous balance sheet records recoverable prepaids and escrows separately when applicable.","Cash-to-close is not computed simply by adding a down payment to a broker's quoted percentage. Earnest money paid before closing reduces the amount still due at settlement, but it does not reduce the total funds the buyer has committed. Seller concessions and lender credits offset eligible charges according to product rules; they are not unrestricted cash paid back to the borrower. A down-payment-assistance second lien changes the financing structure rather than creating free money unless its contract explicitly forgives debt on satisfied conditions.","Suppose the property costs $260,000, the buyer supplies 5% down ($13,000), and the projected transaction charges plus prepaids total a hypothetical $8,000. If earnest money of $3,000 has already been paid, settlement cash is $13,000+$8,000−$3,000=$18,000 before any credits or other adjustments. But the buyer's total committed cash is still $21,000, not $18,000. The correct planning question is how much unrestricted cash remains after the entire transaction, including cash the buyer already deposited.","Professional preparation starts with a uses-and-sources schedule: purchase price, closing and prepaid uses on one side; first mortgage, subordinate financing, verified buyer funds, permitted credits and gifts on the other. The resulting residual must reconcile to the Closing Disclosure. For underwriting, the source of cash matters: moving a large recent deposit into checking does not necessarily make it verified qualifying funds. Students should learn to reconcile all cash movements and then confirm documentary requirements with the lender."],"worked":["Property price $260,000; 5% down $13,000; first mortgage $247,000.","Additional hypothetical closing charges and prepaids $8,000; earnest money already paid $3,000.","Buyer total funding commitment = $13,000+$8,000=$21,000.","Still owed at settlement after earnest money = $18,000, before seller/lender credits.","Opening liquid funds $29,000 before earnest deposit: remaining post-close liquidity $29,000−$21,000=$8,000."],"practice":[["Why is subtracting earnest money from lifetime purchase cost an error?","It reduces the final wire amount because that money has already been paid. It does not make the property or fees cheaper."],["If the $8,000 fee schedule includes $2,000 of recoverable escrow/prepaid assets, how does that affect net worth?","The cash requirement is unchanged; on a simplified opening-to-closing balance sheet only $6,000 of fees are expensed immediately, while the $2,000 remains as a separate asset subject to its terms."]]},{"title":"How down payment percentage changes the funding problem","body":["A larger down payment reduces the starting loan balance and usually reduces leverage. It may change note pricing or mortgage-insurance conditions, but each loan program has its own rules. It also locks up cash that could pay for necessary repairs or protect a household after job loss. The optimization is not simply 'maximize the down payment' or 'minimize the down payment'; it is a constrained decision balancing required cash, note rate, risk, scheduled payments and liquid safety margin.","On a $250,000 home, 5% down is $12,500 and a $237,500 loan; 20% down is $50,000 and a $200,000 loan. At the same hypothetical 6.50% 30-year note rate, monthly principal and interest differ because the loan amount differs. But a true price comparison must also model possible mortgage insurance on the smaller down payment, closing costs, different rate quotes, and the opportunity cost of placing an extra $37,500 into a relatively illiquid asset.","The loan-to-value ratio is calculated from the lender's eligible valuation and lien balances, not from a seller's optimism. If an appraisal supports less than the agreed price and the lender uses the lower valuation, the buyer may need more equity cash or must renegotiate. An extra down payment may solve the financing constraint while destroying necessary reserves. One must calculate both the lender's LTV and post-close cash under alternative appraisals.","Even when a product allows a low down payment, it may have mortgage insurance paid monthly, upfront, or through pricing. Mortgage insurance generally protects against loss to the lender or guarantor. The relevant professional document is a loan-specific cost breakdown, not an assumed universal $100 premium. Borrowers need to ask under which conditions private mortgage insurance can be canceled and how government-backed insurance differs."],"worked":["$250,000 purchase: 5% down = $12,500, financed $237,500; 20% down = $50,000, financed $200,000.","At hypothetical 6.50% fixed for 360 months: P&I ≈$1,501.16 versus $1,264.14.","Payment difference ≈$237.02 per month, excluding insurance, taxes and changes in loan pricing.","Larger down payment consumes $37,500 more at closing, reducing cash available for a reserve.","With an appraisal of $240,000 and $237,500 loan, LTV against the appraisal would be approximately 98.96%; pricing and eligibility must be rechecked."],"practice":[["Does 20% down guarantee the lowest overall five-year cost?","No. It may avoid some forms of mortgage insurance and reduce borrowing, but opportunity costs, rates, fees and sale or refinance timing also matter."],["What is the economic cost of a down payment when the buyer already owns the cash?","It is a transfer into illiquid home equity; the opportunity cost is the foregone flexibility and potential return of the cash, not an immediate $-for-$ loss of wealth."]]},{"title":"Emergency reserves are a separate source-and-use constraint","body":["Liquidity is more than a bank account balance on one day. The relevant amount is unrestricted cash that can be accessed in time to meet necessities, after reserving near-term bills and transaction commitments. Money set aside for rent or mortgage, income taxes, replacement transportation, and known medical spending cannot also be called an emergency fund. A family with a large balance but several imminent obligations may have a much smaller usable reserve than the headline number suggests.","An emergency-fund target can be expressed as E=m×C where m is months of protection and C is essential monthly expenditure. A $3,200 essentials budget calls for $9,600 at three months, assuming roughly stable expenditure. Yet a homeowner may also need a distinct replacement reserve for roof, HVAC, appliance and structural repairs. Reserves can be built in stages but cannot be fabricated in a spreadsheet to make closing feasible.","Consider $29,000 of available cash, $21,000 total home-purchase cash, and a desired $9,600 emergency reserve. Post-closing cash would be $8,000, so the reserve target would be missed by $1,600 before moving or immediate repair costs. If an inspection flags a plausible $3,000 near-term repair, the economic safety gap becomes more acute. The correct advice may be negotiating repairs, selecting lower housing cost, saving additional funds, or adjusting the homebuying schedule.","Do not promise a particular number of months as universally sufficient. A household with two stable earners, strong disability coverage and low fixed expenses faces a different interruption risk from a single seasonal worker supporting dependents. Stress testing uses relevant shocks: wage interruption, insurance deductible, heating-fuel spike, property-tax increase, vehicle repair, or a simultaneous shock. Record which costs can actually be deferred and which cannot."],"worked":["Available bank cash $29,000; total purchase cash commitment $21,000.","Unrestricted cash after closing $8,000, assuming no other earmarks.","Monthly essential spending $3,200; target three-month reserve $9,600.","Reserve shortfall $1,600, before moving and repair expenses.","If a $3,000 immediate repair occurs, remaining cash $5,000, or 1.56 months of the modeled essentials."],"practice":[["Why is unused credit limit not counted in the emergency reserve?","It is conditional borrowing capacity; using it creates debt and future repayment obligations rather than adding net economic assets."],["If essentials rise to $3,600, what is a three-month reserve target?","3×$3,600=$10,800; the same $8,000 post-close cash now covers only 2.22 months."]]},{"title":"Opportunity cost and mortgage insurance: compare the entire system","body":["The cost of the marginal down payment must be compared with the complete change in financing. Paying $10,000 more down typically reduces principal and its contractual interest, but it also reduces liquidity. Depending on lender pricing, the marginal dollars may cross an insurance threshold, improve rate quotes, or fail to change either. In economics, opportunity cost is the value of the best forgone alternative, not necessarily a guaranteed investment return.","A student should construct at least two realistic cash-flow paths. In Path A the household pays the minimum eligible down payment, retains cash, and pays a higher installment and any applicable mortgage insurance. In Path B the household increases the down payment, reducing interest expense but potentially leaving too little after closing. For each path calculate expected post-close reserves, monthly housing expenditure, financial exposure after 24 and 60 months, and plausible forced-sale consequences.","Suppose one path preserves $15,000 cash but requires $1,550 monthly P&I plus hypothetical $130 mortgage insurance; another preserves $5,000 cash but requires $1,420 P&I and no mortgage insurance. Their first-year payment difference is $260 per month, or $3,120 over a simple 12-payment total. Yet the second path holds $10,000 less cash against a $7,000 repair or earnings interruption. Neither number alone determines the appropriate path.","A disciplined sensitivity grid varies interest rates, insurance estimates, maintenance, holding period and investment opportunity cost. Do not claim that a projected investment return is guaranteed; also do not assume home equity is easily borrowable during unemployment or adverse appraisal conditions. The objective is a purchase plan that remains possible under credible departures from the base case."],"worked":["Path A housing debt/insurance ≈$1,550+$130=$1,680 monthly; retained cash $15,000.","Path B housing debt/insurance ≈$1,420+$0=$1,420 monthly; retained cash $5,000.","Path B modeled monthly saving $260, annual $3,120; liquidity sacrifice $10,000.","Simple payoff of $10,000 from $260/month takes about 38.46 months, ignoring loan-balance and time-value differences.","Path B cannot directly pay a $7,000 emergency from its retained cash; the other $2,000 requires a separate funding solution."],"practice":[["Why is a simple payment payback not a complete present-value comparison?","It omits interest timing, principal balances, differing fees, asset returns, opportunity costs and the probability the mortgage ends early."],["How would a lender quote change the model?","Replace hypothetical PMI and rates with actual Loan Estimates, retain identical loan amount and horizon conventions, and recompute the comparison."]]},{"title":"Down payment assistance: gifts, loans, and conditional forgiveness","body":["Assistance programs use different legal instruments. A grant may not require repayment if conditions are satisfied; a deferred loan is still a liability that may become payable at a triggering event; a forgivable loan may discharge principal over a vesting schedule; a repayable second mortgage can require installments alongside the first loan. These distinctions matter for the household balance sheet and later refinancing or sale.","Connecticut CHFA's publicly posted Time To Own information, reviewed November 1, 2026, describes a temporary 0% interest subordinate loan without monthly payments, up to $25,000, with 10% of original principal forgiven annually over ten years if contractual conditions are met. It requires a qualifying CHFA first mortgage and other eligibility requirements, including residency. Published remaining funds as of October 26, 2026 are a dated snapshot, not a reservation or guarantee of availability. Never treat conditional forgiveness as already-earned household wealth.","CHFA's separate DAP program is described as an interest-bearing second mortgage whose terms and amount differ from Time To Own. A borrower must compare first-mortgage pricing, assistance debt, total liens, monthly payments, underwriting and the consequences of moving, selling or refinancing. The same home can have very different balance-sheet and cash-flow consequences under a private gift, forgivable loan and repayable subordinate mortgage.","An ethical planner never infers eligibility from a state-residence checkbox alone. Verify current funding, program reservation rules, income limits, targeted areas, required homebuyer education, borrower contribution, allowable costs, occupancy, forgiveness triggers and interest/repayment consequences with a participating lender and the controlling program documents. If a brochure and later bulletin conflict, the current controlling guideline requires clarification before using it in a purchase decision."],"worked":["Hypothetical Time To Own-style subordinate loan principal $20,000; assume linear forgiveness of 10% of ORIGINAL loan each annual anniversary, subject to contract.","Immediately after closing the entire $20,000 is a liability, not cash profit.","After 3 qualifying annual forgiveness events, modeled forgiven principal $6,000; unforgiven $14,000.","If the home is sold before full forgiveness, determine the current legal payoff and whether a triggering event requires repayment; don't assume the entire $20,000 vanished.","Illustrative program maximum does not determine how much this buyer qualifies to receive or whether funds remain available."],"practice":[["Why is a zero-monthly-payment second lien not economically equivalent to a gift?","The subordinate obligation can remain repayable, affect refinancing/sale and create collateral constraints until forgiveness or lawful discharge."],["What is the balance of a $20,000 loan after exactly five qualifying annual 10%-of-original forgiveness events?","$20,000−5×$2,000=$10,000, assuming the program's specific conditions are satisfied."]]},{"title":"Funding decision memorandum and independent assessment","body":["A closing plan should be judged both by sources-and-uses reconciliation and by resilience after settlement. The worksheet starts with a dated deposit and credit history, then itemizes earnest money, down payment, lender and third-party fees, prepaids, escrows, moving costs, reserves and conditional assistance. It then tests whether the buyer can both bring the amount due and keep essential cash available without assuming unspecified new lending.","Suppose a household holds $29,000 and evaluates a $260,000 property with $13,000 down, $8,000 in all modeled settlement charges, $3,000 already paid as earnest deposit, and $3,000 immediate repair risk. The exact wire at closing could be $18,000, but the transaction uses $21,000 overall and the repair could leave only $5,000. The student should not mistake the smaller wire figure for a larger cash reserve.","A professional-grade alternative uses an explicit decision tree: buy now with written concessions; request documented and available assistance; choose another house; or wait while building liquidity. Each path should include a constraint test and a numerical downside. Qualitative preferences such as stability, school district and access to work matter, but the memo should state their value in words instead of fabricating cash savings.","Finish by identifying gaps in evidence. A lender must confirm qualifying assets and assistance. A home inspector evaluates condition within the scope of inspection, while specialized contractors may be needed. An insurance professional quotes replacement and liability coverage. A settlement lawyer explains title and closing mechanics. The learner's job is to integrate the evidence and identify the limits of the proposed purchase."],"worked":["Commitment schedule: down payment $13,000 + settlement costs $8,000 = total $21,000.","After $3,000 previously paid earnest deposit, final wire $18,000; no expense disappears.","Post-close cash $29,000−$21,000=$8,000.","After modeled near-term $3,000 repair, unrestricted remaining cash $5,000.","If essential monthly costs are $3,200, stressed runway $5,000/$3,200≈1.56 months, before any additional shock."],"practice":[["Produce a two-option comparison that preserves at least $10,000 of cash.","Explain which purchase, credit, concession or saving adjustment could make the constraint feasible; specify which assumption is conditional."],["Why does a home inspection not guarantee affordable ownership?","Inspections have limits, hidden defects can exist, and even known future maintenance or insurance costs may exceed the household's available cash."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CFPB · Loan Estimate","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"],["CFPB · Loan comparison","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/compare/compare-loan-estimates/"],["CHFA · Time To Own 2026","https://www.chfa.org/homebuyers-homeowners/homebuyers/time-to-own-down-payment-assistance-program-loan/"],["CHFA · Down Payment Assistance Program","https://www.chfa.org/homebuyers-homeowners/homebuyers/downpayment-assistance-program-dap-loan/"],["CFPB · Loan-to-value and mortgage insurance","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-loan-to-value-ratio-and-how-does-it-relate-to-my-costs-en-121/"]],"assessment":{"title":"Cash-to-close versus liquidity case","prompt":"Reconcile a $260,000 purchase with $29,000 starting liquid cash, 5% down, $8,000 settlement charges, a $3,000 already-paid deposit, and a $3,000 immediate repair. Produce a sources-and-uses schedule and test three-month reserves for $3,200 essential monthly spending. Compare one alternative and discuss funding documentation and possible CHFA assistance without assuming approval.","rubric":["Cash-to-close and earnest-money reconciliation (25%)","Unrestricted reserve calculation and shock (25%)","LTV and opportunity cost (20%)","Assistance loan legal classification and documented conditions (15%)","Clear evidence and decision memo (15%)"]}},{"id":"L2.S3","title":"The Anatomy of a Mortgage","subtitle":"Amortization, APR, points, escrow and refinance mathematics","prerequisites":["L1.S3 present value","L1.S4 debt amortization","L2.S1 secured financing"],"objectives":["Recreate first-year and five-year amortization balances","Distinguish note rate, APR and the contractual payment","Calculate points and lender credit break-even under multiple holding periods","Read Loan Estimate and Closing Disclosure evidence","Model escrow, adjustable-rate and refinance shocks"],"sections":[{"title":"Cash-flow timelines and the balance recursion","body":["To evaluate a mortgage, identify the date and contractual purpose of each cash flow. At closing, the borrower receives mortgage financing on behalf of the purchase and pays the required down payment and settlement costs. In each period, the homeowner pays principal and interest under the note, plus property-related charges that may be collected through escrow. The economic cost of owning and occupying the property also includes maintenance, capital replacements, utilities and market risks that do not appear in the note.","Amortization is best understood as a recurrence, B_t=B_(t−1)(1+r)−A. Here B_0 is principal, r is the note's periodic interest rate, A is the scheduled debt installment, and B_t is the remaining balance after payment t. The monthly interest charge on opening principal B_(t−1) is r times that balance; the difference A−rB_(t−1) reduces principal. As principal falls, future interest usually falls under a fixed rate and constant payment.","The closed-form solution B_t=P(1+r)^t−A[((1+r)^t−1)/r] follows by repeatedly substituting the recurrence. This makes a practical verification test: a spreadsheet running row-by-row should match the closed form at month 12, month 60 and maturity within rounding conventions. The formula does not apply unchanged to adjustable-rate resets, balloon features, extra payments, fees financed into principal, or loans with irregular payment conventions.","Loan maturity is not the same as investment holding period. A homeowner may sell or refinance in year three or seven. To compare two mortgage offers, model the payment history during the chosen horizon, the balance that must be settled then, and the upfront fees. Computing only a payment difference can miss different equity accumulation and a large cash fee paid at origination."],"worked":["P=$237,500, hypothetical fixed note rate 6.50%, n=360, monthly payment ≈$1,501.16.","First-month interest ≈$1,286.46, principal repaid ≈$214.70.","After 12 payments balance ≈$234,845.40; the buyer repaid ≈$2,654.60 of original principal.","After 60 payments balance ≈$222,326.07; first 60 P&I installments total approximately $90,069.69.","Five-year payoff would also reflect statement timing, potential accrued interest and applicable charges under actual terms."],"practice":[["What does mortgage amortization demonstrate about equity accumulation?","Amortization reduces debt on schedule; home equity also depends on property value, other liens and costs of selling."],["State a test for a spreadsheet with inconsistent ending balances.","Compare recursive and closed-form debt balances at month 12, 60 and final maturity, and verify units for the rate and number of periods."]]},{"title":"Interest rate and APR describe different prices","body":["The note rate is the contractual rate that usually determines the scheduled interest charge. APR is a broader regulatory price measure that incorporates specified finance charges and the timing of funds according to disclosure rules. Two loans can have equal note rates but different APRs if their qualifying upfront charges differ. Conversely, a lower note rate purchased with expensive points may produce a higher APR or be economically inferior over the borrower's actual holding period.","APR is not a substitute for a full household ownership budget because property taxes, maintenance and many insurance items are distinct from the regulated finance charge. It is also not a prediction of the maximum future rate on an adjustable-rate mortgage. Rather than compute APR by casually dividing fees by principal, professionals use the proper dated cash-flow and disclosure conventions and compare the Loan Estimate's APR on page three.","In a simplified educational effective-yield illustration, a lender advances net borrower proceeds P−F where F is finance charge included upfront and receives A each month. The periodic internal rate i satisfies P−F=sum(A/(1+i)^t) over all n payments, assuming these are the only included cash flows. The resulting annualized i is a useful economic intuition, not a legally compliant replacement for the APR rules used on official disclosures.","If a salesperson promises a low interest rate, insist on the same loan amount, rate lock, term, discount points, origination fees, closing expense and required mortgage insurance assumptions for all comparisons. When different terms create a lower monthly bill, determine whether the saving arises from genuinely lower price, added fee, higher risk, longer repayment or loan balance restructuring."],"worked":["Offer A hypothetical 6.50% note rate; Offer B hypothetical 6.25% note rate; same principal $237,500 and 360 months.","P&I payments ≈$1,501.16 and ≈$1,462.33; difference ≈$38.83 per month.","If Offer B requires $2,375 in discount points, simple cash-payment payback is $2,375/$38.83≈61.2 months.","This payback ignores the lower unpaid loan balance on Offer B and discounting, so it is a quick screening metric only.","Review the lender-provided APR and line-item fees on each actual Loan Estimate rather than declaring one APR from this incomplete data."],"practice":[["Why can't mortgage APR simply equal interest rate plus fees divided by principal?","APR reflects timed finance charges and prescribed disclosure conventions; the naive fraction ignores payment timing, term and fee treatment."],["When does an APR comparison require special care?","When terms, adjustable-rate features, initial costs, expected payoff dates or included finance charges differ."]]},{"title":"An economically correct five-year comparison","body":["Loan offers should be compared over a defined planning horizon, not an arbitrary full contract term. A present-value analysis uses a common discount rate for all candidates and accounts for cash today, each payment, and payoff or remaining balance at the horizon. For a homeowner selling after five years, a loan with a larger unpaid balance requires a larger payoff from sale proceeds, even if its monthly payment was lower.","Define undiscounted five-year outlay C_60=upfront lender fees +sum of first 60 scheduled P&I installments+unpaid loan balance after month 60. This expression is useful when comparing offers on the same property and principal with no prepayment fee, excluding common property expenses and assuming payoff exactly at installment 60. It combines payment outlay with remaining principal. Because both offers financed the same original principal, the difference isolates five-year financing costs under the model.","Using a 6.50% loan with no extra point charge and a 6.25% loan with $2,375 points, the lower-rate offer has smaller installments and a slightly smaller unpaid balance after five years. The cumulative five-year financing outlay saving is about $605 under the assumptions. The simple $38.83-monthly savings produce only $2,329.98 over 60 months, apparently less than the points, yet the extra $650.12 of principal reduction changes the conclusion; this is why balance comparison is mandatory.","The five-year dollar arithmetic still lacks time value. For a rigorous NPV, choose a discount rate, place fees at time zero, monthly differences on dates 1–60, and the payoff-balance difference at month 60; discount each separately. Test horizons of 24, 60 and 120 months and vary the discount rate. A conclusion can reverse with different holding periods. The procedure is general; do not confuse the illustrative offers with current Connecticut rate quotes."],"worked":["Offer A 6.50%: 60 payments × $1,501.16156 + balance $222,326.07 ≈ $312,395.76.","Offer B 6.25%: 60 payments × $1,462.32835 + balance $221,675.95 + points $2,375 ≈ $311,790.65.","Five-year undiscounted advantage for B ≈$605.12, under the precise horizon and fee assumptions.","A 24-month horizon must be computed separately; do not scale the five-year result linearly.","Use a discount rate consistent with the timing and risk of the alternatives if comparing present-value costs."],"practice":[["Why is the 60-month balance difference economically material?","A lower outstanding principal means less payoff at sale or refinance and higher equity for the same property value."],["How does a possible refinance after 18 months affect prepaid points?","The benefit horizon is shortened; the upfront cost may not be earned back by reduced payments and principal."]]},{"title":"Escrow, insurance and the full housing bill","body":["Mortgage escrow holds funds collected by a servicer to pay specified bills such as property taxes and hazard insurance. It is not extra loan principal, nor a permanent guaranteed monthly price. A servicer's annual analysis reviews actual disbursements, forecast bills, allowable cushion and any shortage or surplus. Taxes and insurance can rise independently of the fixed note-rate payment. Mortgage insurance is another category with its own cancellation and pricing rules.","A simple initial budget computes escrow collection as the annual projected property taxes plus eligible insurance charges divided by twelve, with a separately identified initial deposit or cushion according to applicable rules. A $5,400 annual tax estimate and $1,800 annual hazard premium suggest $600 per month toward those two categories. The actual monthly collection may also reflect escrow account shortages and approved cushion calculations, and the closing wire may include prepaid insurance and an initial escrow deposit.","If taxes rise by $900 and annual insurance by $300, the projected annual disbursement increases by $1,200, or $100 per month, before any shortage repayment. A $1,200 shortage amortized over twelve months would add another $100 per month during the shortage repayment period if that is the applicable servicing treatment. Thus a household might see an approximately $200 total monthly increase in that illustrative scenario while the fixed-rate P&I remains constant.","Homeownership budgeting must further include maintenance, replacement of durable components, utilities, association assessments and property-level risks such as flood exclusions. Calling an entire lender-collected payment 'mortgage interest' is an accounting mistake. Evaluate each component's timing, control, tax treatment and volatility separately so the family can locate the source of unexpected changes."],"worked":["P&I ≈$1,501.16; projected tax $5,400/year = $450/month; hazard insurance $1,800/year = $150/month.","Initial simplified principal-interest-tax-insurance total ≈$2,101.16, excluding mortgage insurance and maintenance.","Tax increases $900/year, insurance $300/year ⇒ extra ongoing escrow need $100/month.","Illustrative escrow shortage $1,200 paid in 12 equal increments ⇒ temporary $100/month more, subject to servicing rules.","Revised monthly billed amount could approximate $2,301.16 during the shortage period before other items; the fixed P&I component did not change."],"practice":[["Does a fixed-rate mortgage guarantee the entire housing payment stays constant?","No. Principal-and-interest can stay constant while escrow, mortgage insurance, HOA, maintenance and other property costs change."],["What records should be reviewed for an escrow increase?","Annual escrow analysis, disbursement statement, property tax bill, insurer renewal, initial and current escrow balances."]]},{"title":"Loan Estimate, Closing Disclosure and transaction audit","body":["The Loan Estimate is generally a three-page standardized form for covered mortgages and supplies early loan terms, estimated payments and settlement charges. After the relevant application elements are received, federal rules generally require delivery or mailing within three business days. The Closing Disclosure is generally a five-page form supplied near closing, with receipt at least three business days before consummation. Exceptions exist for certain product types; reviewing the source's exclusions is part of professional literacy.","An audit is a line-by-line reconstruction. On page one verify note rate, payment, loan term, payment changes, mortgage insurance and estimated escrow. On the costs pages review origination charges, third-party services, taxes and filing charges, prepaid interest and insurance, initial escrow deposit, seller concessions, lender credits, and the path from closing costs to cash to close. Verify rate lock and loan-program terms rather than relying on an unrecorded conversation.","The documents' legal meanings differ. The deed establishes the property interest conveyed, the note embodies a repayment obligation, the mortgage establishes security rights, the Closing Disclosure details the transaction, and title insurance has policy-specific coverage and exceptions. Legal questions about joint vesting, trusts, liens and probate should be reviewed by qualified counsel rather than solved from a calculator.","A meaningful assessment asks the learner to locate a red flag: a disclosed monthly payment excluding substantial taxes, an unexpected adjustable-rate product, discount points that were not expected, seller credits that do not reduce the final amount as predicted, or an escrow figure inconsistent with public taxes. The corrective action is to seek an updated explanation or corrected documents before closing, not to silently override the numbers."],"worked":["Hypothetical initial Loan Estimate: note rate 6.50%, 30-year fixed, down payment $13,000, $8,000 closing/prepaid costs.","Hypothetical corrected Closing Disclosure: rate 6.75%, additional $2,000 charge, different estimated taxes.","Flag rate/fee changes; determine whether the rate lock, changed circumstances and tolerance rules justify them.","Recalculate P&I on the actual loan principal; separately reconcile cash-to-close, escrow and prepaids.","Do not sign based solely on the initial estimate; obtain written resolution and legal/consumer guidance when needed."],"practice":[["Is a verbal 'no closing cost' promise enough to compare lenders?","No. Review the disclosed lender credits, rate, financed fees, total closing costs, cash to close and future payments."],["Which page has the Loan Estimate's APR comparison?","The CFPB identifies APR in the Loan Estimate's comparisons area on page 3."]]},{"title":"Adjustable rates, refinance and remaining term","body":["Adjustable-rate mortgages generally link future note rates to a specified index plus margin, with contractual adjustment dates, periodic caps and lifetime caps. The initial rate can be attractive while exposing the borrower to later payment shocks. A cautious student must read adjustment terms, not assume the rate will move with a single headline Federal Reserve policy change. Payment caps and negative-amortization features require additional analysis of the product documents.","To model refinancing, start with the current payoff balance B_k, not the original principal. Compute the current required payment for the remaining term and the proposed new payment and fees for the new term. A refinance from a 26-year remaining loan to a new 30-year loan can lower monthly payments simply by stretching debt longer. Compare remaining-interest cost, upfront fees, liquidity, tax considerations, repayment horizon and the risk of moving before the apparent savings accumulate.","Consider an original $240,000 loan at a hypothetical 7.00% fixed over 360 months. After 48 payments the balance is approximately $229,138.91. Refinance that balance at a hypothetical 6.00% for 30 new years, with $4,000 in refinancing costs paid in cash. The original scheduled monthly P&I was about $1,596.73; the new P&I must be calculated on the remaining $229,138.91, not the original $240,000. A borrower might regain cash-flow flexibility while lengthening the contractual payoff date by four years.","The professional answer is not simply 'refinance whenever the rate falls.' A higher new loan balance from financed fees, foreclosure or escrow constraints, a near-term move, poor credit, shorter remaining term or a lost assistance loan could outweigh headline savings. Model the refinance with at least three payoff dates and disclose every included and excluded cost."],"worked":["Original loan $240,000 at 7.00% over 360 months: original P&I ≈$1,596.73.","After 48 on-time installments, modeled balance ≈$229,138.91.","Refinanced balance $229,138.91 at 6.00%, new 360-month term ⇒ new P&I approximately $1,373.80.","Monthly difference ≈$222.92; $4,000 cash closing fee gives naive payment payback ≈17.94 months.","Yet maturity moves 48 months later, and new-vs-old unpaid balances must be compared at the planned sale horizon."],"practice":[["Why does refinancing not necessarily reduce total remaining interest?","Restarting a longer term can reduce monthly payment while extending the number of interest-bearing periods; fees and payoff timing matter."],["What is the appropriate principal for the refinance calculation?","The documented payoff/current financed amount, adjusted for any rolled-in costs and other financing terms, not the loan's original amount."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CFPB · Note rate vs APR","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-the-difference-between-a-mortgage-interest-rate-and-an-apr-en-135/"],["CFPB · Points and lender credits","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/how-should-i-use-lender-credits-and-points-also-called-discount-points-en-136/"],["CFPB · Compare and negotiate Loan Estimates","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/compare/compare-loan-estimates/"],["CFPB · Closing Disclosure guide","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/closing-disclosure/"],["CFPB · Escrow annual changes","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-an-escrow-or-impound-account-en-140/"]],"assessment":{"title":"Mortgage offer forensic review","prompt":"Calculate a 60-month total financing comparison of $237,500 loans at 6.50% with zero points and 6.25% with $2,375 points. Show balances, not only payment savings. Then prepare a simulated closing-document change log and analyze a 48-month refinance with a new 30-year term and $4,000 fees.","rubric":["Correct amortization and balance model (30%)","Points and hold-period comparison (25%)","Escrow and disclosure literacy (20%)","Refinance NPV/term tradeoff (15%)","Evidence and limitations (10%)"]}},{"id":"L2.S4","title":"Property Taxes by Location","subtitle":"Connecticut assessments, mill rates, escrow and housing affordability","prerequisites":["L2.S1 cash-flow components","L2.S3 mortgage escrow","L1.S2 monthly/annual conversions"],"objectives":["Calculate Connecticut tax bills from taxable assessment and applicable municipal rates","Distinguish appraisal value, assessment and purchase price","Model revaluation versus mill-rate changes without claiming one-for-one movement","Evaluate district charges, appeals, exemptions and billing schedules","Integrate taxes into escrow, mortgage underwriting and rent-versus-buy decisions"],"sections":[{"title":"Property-tax architecture: value, assessment, rate and levy","body":["Property tax is a recurring charge on real property imposed principally by local taxing authorities. It can help fund schools, emergency services, infrastructure and other municipal purposes. In Connecticut, local assessors establish taxable assessments and municipalities determine mill rates within the applicable legal process. A mill means one dollar of tax for every $1,000 of assessed taxable value. Statewide headlines about 'Connecticut taxes' cannot replace a property-specific calculation.","Connecticut General Statutes section 12-62a describes a general assessment ratio of 70% of present true and actual value, subject to legal provisions and exceptions. The home's listing price, contract price, bank appraisal and municipal assessed value may be materially different. The actual tax bill is computed from the certified assessment, applicable mill rates, charges, exemptions and special-district rules; multiplying the newly negotiated purchase price by a generic rate is only a scenario.","Under the simple textbook model, taxable assessed value V_a=market value×0.70 and annual base property tax T=V_a×mill rate/1,000. For a hypothetical market value of $245,000 and a hypothetical 34.00-mill rate, assessment is $171,500 and tax $5,831 annually, or about $485.92 per month. This is an illustration—not the current Vernon, Rockville, or another municipality's rate.","Professional work documents the valuation date, assessment date, current fiscal-year rate, location and any district levies. A property straddling districts or subject to special assessments may owe more. Exemptions and phase-ins can change the final tax. Before purchasing, obtain the actual parcel assessment and bill from the municipality, and verify that a revaluation or new budget has not changed the estimates after the seller's last bill."],"worked":["Hypothetical market value $245,000; general assessment assumption 70% ⇒ assessed value $171,500.","Hypothetical rate 34 mills means $34 for each $1,000 assessed.","Annual base tax $171,500×34/1,000 = $5,831.","Monthly reserve equivalent $5,831/12 ≈$485.92.","This is not a current bill for any named town; use local assessor and collector data for an actual property."],"practice":[["What is 1 mill in tax terms?","One dollar of tax per $1,000 of taxable assessment, not 1% of market value."],["What happens if the assessed value is $180,000 and total applicable rate is 30 mills?","Annual simplified tax = $180,000×30/1,000=$5,400, before any adjustments."]]},{"title":"Revaluation is not equivalent to a proportional tax increase","body":["A town periodically revalues taxable property to reflect legal assessment requirements and market changes. If every parcel's assessed value increases at the same rate, a municipality could reduce its mill rate and raise the same total levy; individual bills would not necessarily rise in the proportion of the appraisal increase. If one property appreciates faster than the tax base as a whole, it could bear a larger share even when the municipal levy stays constant.","Separate the numerator and denominator. Let municipality-wide taxable grand list be G and the property-tax revenue requirement be L. A simplified mill rate that raises precisely that levy is m=1,000×L/G. Increasing G while L remains constant reduces the needed m. A parcel's share of the levy can be approximated by its own assessment divided by G. In real systems, district rates, nonproperty revenues, delinquency, exemptions and budget decisions complicate the simple equation.","Consider a town with $1 billion taxable assessed value and a required $30 million levy: m=30 mills. If the grand list rises to $1.20 billion while the desired levy remains $30 million, a simple adjusted rate would be 25 mills. A parcel rising from $200,000 assessment to $240,000 assessment pays $6,000 both before and after. But if its value rises to $260,000 while the overall base rises 20%, its new bill would be $6,500 at 25 mills.","An analyst should not forecast a tax bill from a home's Zillow estimate or from the percent change in a particular assessment alone. Use the latest local grand-list assessment, adopted mill rate, exemption eligibility and notice for the new fiscal year. Where revaluation is scheduled, present plausible ranges and identify the actual budget and assessment decisions still unknown."],"worked":["Opening municipal assessed tax base G=$1,000,000,000; target levy L=$30,000,000 ⇒ rate m=30 mills.","Revalued G=$1,200,000,000 with levy unchanged ⇒ m=25 mills.","Parcel A: $200,000 prior assessment×30/1,000=$6,000.","Parcel A grows 20% to $240,000×25/1,000=$6,000; no increase under this model.","Parcel B grows to $260,000×25/1,000=$6,500, so relative valuation changes matter."],"practice":[["Does a 20% reassessment imply a 20% higher tax bill?","No. The final bill also depends on new mill rates, levy requirements and the property's relative valuation."],["If a levy rises to $33 million with a $1.2 billion grand list, calculate the simplified rate.","1000×33,000,000/1,200,000,000=27.5 mills."]]},{"title":"What the bill does and does not tell a buyer","body":["Property taxes often appear in escrow estimates on a lender's Loan Estimate. Yet the seller's last bill may reflect a previous assessment, special exemption, assessment schedule, or taxing district. A buyer should verify the property's address, map/block/lot or parcel identity, taxable assessment, tax bill period, special assessments and whether any exemptions terminate after transfer. Municipal websites and assessor or tax collector records are primary sources.","An annual bill and its due-date installments are different from the lender's monthly escrow contributions. A household may face semiannual local tax due dates while depositing one-twelfth of estimated annual tax into its mortgage escrow each month. Closing proration between buyer and seller settles responsibility for a portion of a period and must not be interpreted as a permanent lower tax rate.","An appeal challenges assessment according to defined municipal and state procedures; it is not a guarantee of reduction merely because a homeowner dislikes the bill. Students must identify the proper authority, deadlines, admissible comparable-property evidence and whether a successful appeal affects past or future assessments. Legal exemptions and relief programs require eligibility, documentation and often annual or periodic verification.","Property tax is an operating cost of housing. It matters for lender qualifying payments, household cash-flow capacity, rent-versus-buy comparisons, and resale expectations. An expensive home in a lower-mill-rate jurisdiction is not automatically cheaper to own than a less expensive home elsewhere. Compare actual assessment, rate, tax, insurance, utilities, HOA, commuting and maintenance rather than rank towns on a single headline mill rate."],"worked":["House A: hypothetical assessed $175,000 at 35 mills ⇒ annual base tax $6,125.","House B: hypothetical assessed $220,000 at 28 mills ⇒ annual base tax $6,160.","Despite House B's lower rate, its property tax is $35 higher each year because the assessment is greater.","If House A has special-district 2 mills, add $350/year on the same assumed assessment, yielding $6,475 combined.","Verify if the special district applies to the parcel before treating either total as an actual bill."],"practice":[["Which property has lower tax: $175,000 assessment at 35 mills versus $220,000 at 28 mills?","Before district charges, $6,125 versus $6,160; first is lower by $35."],["Why should the buyer ask about an existing exemption?","The seller's preferential assessment or exemption may not carry over to a new owner's eligibility."]]},{"title":"Tax escrow forecasting and multi-year stress","body":["Escrow forecasting starts with the amount and timing of anticipated property-related disbursements, not simply the month's cash balance. In many covered mortgage escrow accounts, the servicer can collect one-twelfth of anticipated annual expenses each month and maintain an allowable cushion under Regulation X. Annual analysis identifies shortages and surpluses based on a trial running balance. The servicer's calculation should be reconciled against actual tax and insurance bills.","Assume annual tax $5,831 and annual homeowners insurance $1,800. The simplified monthly funding baseline is ($5,831+$1,800)/12=$635.92. If the tax rises 12% and insurance 20%, next year's tax is $6,530.72 and insurance $2,160; ongoing annual disbursements grow to $8,690.72, corresponding to $724.23 a month. This is already an $88.31 increase before an old escrow shortage is recovered.","Now add the possibility of a $600 escrow shortage after annual analysis. If handled through twelve equal extra installments, the temporary payment increment could be $50 a month, giving a modeled escrow line of $774.23 during recovery. Servicing regulations and the actual analysis determine the legally permissible treatment. A fixed-rate note payment remains the same, but the household's transfer to the servicer can still grow.","Professional projection uses low/base/high tax scenarios and annual renewal dates. If a household has only $100 of monthly excess cash, a $138.31 modeled increase in escrow demand would turn the cash flow negative, even with no new borrowing. A home affordability memo should therefore include revaluation sensitivity, hazard premium changes, escrow shortage risk and margin to absorb them."],"worked":["Tax $5,831 + insurance $1,800 ⇒ annual $7,631; monthly $635.92.","Tax increases 12% to $6,530.72; insurance increases 20% to $2,160.","New annual disbursements $8,690.72; monthly baseline $724.23; increase $88.31.","If a modeled $600 prior shortage is collected over 12 months, add $50 temporarily.","Estimated escrow collection during shortage recovery $774.23, excluding other escrowed items."],"practice":[["If monthly cash margin before escrow change was $100, what is it after a $138.31 increase?","Approximately −$38.31 per month, requiring a feasible adjustment."],["Is the escrow cushion an annual tax paid to the government?","No. It is an account balance held for specified projected disbursements under applicable servicing rules."]]},{"title":"Source investigation and the property-tax underwriting memo","body":["A defensible tax forecast starts with official source retrieval. Identify the property parcel at the municipality assessor's site, copy its certified assessed value, locate the current fiscal-year adopted real-property mill rate on the municipality or CT Office of Policy and Management website, and inspect any special districts. State explicitly whether the figure is current, prior-year, proposed or hypothetical. A buyer cannot verify taxes from a stale advertisement alone.","Link property tax to the proposed mortgage: principal and interest under the note, one-twelfth of taxes, one-twelfth of homeowners insurance, any mortgage insurance, and an allowance for maintenance. Then divide the resulting housing obligation by qualifying gross income for a provisional lender-oriented picture, but separately compare it with net household cash and reserve goals. The model must also show a future tax scenario.","Advanced analysis asks why two otherwise similar homes could have different assessed values and bills. Differences may arise from land and improvements, revaluation dates, district boundaries, exemptions, appeals or data errors. Do not use identity or demographics as a substitute for parcel evidence. The ethical role of a financial analyst is to disclose uncertainty and document the correct public data.","For the final assignment compare two hypothetical towns from the same market area with actual published rates once the learner obtains them. If the rates cannot be verified, leave a clearly labelled data slot rather than invent numbers. Produce a reproducible formula sheet, full housing cost forecast, source date, and risk explanation."],"worked":["Candidate price $245,000; placeholder market value $245,000; modeled assessed value 70% = $171,500.","At fictional 34 mills, tax $5,831 annually and $485.92 monthly.","If the actual parcel assessment is $180,000 and actual combined adopted rate is 31 mills, estimated tax is instead $5,580.","New monthly tax reserve $465, or $20.92 less than the original hypothetical scenario.","The verified parcel and adopted rate supersede the generic assumptions."],"practice":[["Name the minimum source fields for a defensible Connecticut tax calculation.","Parcel location/ID, taxable assessed value, applicable adopted real-estate mill rate(s), fiscal period, special levies/exemptions and source date."],["Explain why a property tax forecast can change after contract signing.","Updated assessment, rate adoption, special charges or changed exemption status can change future liability."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["Connecticut General Statutes §12-62a · assessment ratio","https://www.cga.ct.gov/2025/pub/chap_203.htm"],["Connecticut OPM · official mill rate tables","https://portal.ct.gov/opm/igpp/publications/mill-rates"],["CT DRS · 2025 Tax Incidence Study, property taxes","https://portal.ct.gov/-/media/drs/drs-reports/ct-tax-incidence-study-2025-drs.pdf"],["CFPB · Mortgage escrow rules and increases","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/mortgage-resources/mortserv/mortgage-servicing-faqs/"],["CFPB · Loan Estimate tax and insurance charges","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"]],"assessment":{"title":"Municipal property-tax investigation","prompt":"Build two comparative taxable-property schedules using a source-dated municipality assessor record and adopted mill rate for each scenario. Separate actual source figures from the fictitious 34-mill demonstration; perform base, 12%-tax shock and escrow-shortage scenarios. Write a memo explaining the difference between a revaluation, a new mill rate and a tax bill.","rubric":["Actual source provenance and date (25%)","Assessment/rate calculation (25%)","Tax and escrow multi-year sensitivity (25%)","Municipal levy reasoning (15%)","Disclosure of uncertainty and exceptions (10%)"]}},{"id":"L2.S5","title":"State Assistance, Refinancing & Trusts","subtitle":"Connecticut CHFA, conditional second liens, mortgage exits and inheritance","prerequisites":["L2.S1 lien and collateral mechanics","L2.S2 reserve and assistance economics","L2.S3 refinance modeling","L2.S4 property tax"],"objectives":["Distinguish CHFA first financing, DAP and Time To Own contract features","Build a conditional loan-forgiveness and early-sale payoff schedule","Compare refinance cash flow with total economic cost including subordinate liens","Identify due-on-sale versus separate assistance-program transfer restrictions","Integrate title, trust, beneficiary coordination and inheritance risks into home purchase planning"],"sections":[{"title":"What Connecticut housing assistance actually finances","body":["Homebuyer assistance is not a single pot of unrestricted money. Connecticut Housing Finance Authority (CHFA) programs involve a qualifying first mortgage from a participating lender and, in some cases, a subordinate loan for down payment or allowable settlement costs. The borrower must qualify under the particular first-mortgage rules and assistance product requirements. A program can be announced yet have limited funds, changing reservation conditions and borrower-specific underwriting requirements.","CHFA's Time To Own public page, reviewed November 1, 2026, describes temporary subordinate financing of up to $25,000 at zero interest with no required monthly payment, scheduled forgiveness of 10% of original principal on each closing anniversary for ten years, provided all requirements are satisfied. Its page reports an additional $5 million authorized September 8 and $12,269,286 available for reservations as of October 26, 2026. Those are dated public snapshots, not confirmation that any specific purchase can reserve funding now.","The CHFA Downpayment Assistance Program (DAP) is a different product described as an interest-bearing second mortgage with its own stated limits, rate and underwriting. Program maximums, purchase-price limits, income tests, borrower contribution rules, education requirements, geographic categories and subordination restrictions can change. A borrowing plan needs a source-dated comparison and a lender-produced combined financing disclosure rather than generic internet advice.","Professionals explicitly distinguish a program benefit from a guaranteed benefit. A hypothetical $20,000 Time To Own loan may lower buyer cash required at closing, but the original subordinate claim is still debt and can affect sale or refinancing. Financial literacy requires understanding both the favorable structure and the conditions under which the arrangement becomes less favorable. A gift and a contingent-forgiveness lien should never be counted as the same economic transaction."],"worked":["Illustrative Connecticut purchase $250,000, first mortgage $237,500, required down payment $12,500 plus $7,500 modeled closing costs.","Without assistance, stated upfront transaction cash $20,000.","If a fully eligible $20,000 subordinate assistance loan is actually available and allowed for these costs, cash at closing could be substantially reduced, subject to program/lender rules and other charges.","At closing, balance sheet still records a $20,000 second-lien liability until portions are formally forgiven.","Loan closing cannot be planned as funded until eligibility, amount, timing and reservation are documented."],"practice":[["Why is current published program funding not a personal approval?","Funds can be reserved by others, rules and availability change, and applicant, property and first-loan eligibility require independent underwriting."],["Explain why an assistance lien may increase combined loan-to-value.","First and second mortgage claims are both obligations secured by property; combined collateral exposure includes subordinate debt."]]},{"title":"Model the ten-year forgiveness schedule and triggering events","body":["Forgiveness schedules should be modeled as dated transactions, not as a story that the government gives the borrower immediate wealth. For a hypothetical $20,000 original Time To Own loan with 10% of original principal forgiven on each qualifying closing anniversary, each anniversary removes $2,000 of the liability. Before the first anniversary, the full $20,000 remains. Immediately after the third qualifying anniversary, $14,000 remains; after the fifth, $10,000 remains; after the tenth, zero remains, assuming the contract permits and all conditions are met.","CHFA's borrower-facing Time To Own notice states that the loan can become due on sale or transfer, loss of principal-residence status, refinance of the first mortgage, or at the end of ten years. Because program bulletins can update forms, the learner must review the actually executed note, mortgage, notice and current lender instructions before predicting payoff or tax results. A sale after four years may require the unpaid balance at that date, while a sale after full earned forgiveness may not.","Suppose the home appreciates from $250,000 to a hypothetical $280,000 and the first loan has been paid down to $225,000. If the household sells just after its third forgiveness anniversary, and the second lien remaining is $14,000, equity before selling costs would be $280,000−$225,000−$14,000=$41,000. A 6% illustrative selling cost would reduce that result by $16,800, leaving $24,200 in modeled gross sale proceeds after liens and costs. The amount of taxable gain or taxes due is a separate question, not necessarily equal to sale proceeds.","A plan involving inheritances or trusts must also account for the second lien's transfer conditions. A transfer that is exempt from a first mortgage's due-on-sale clause may nevertheless be restricted by the subordinate assistance contract. This is a legal-document verification task, not a claim that every trust transfer is prohibited. It is especially important when a borrower expects to buy immediately and transfer title to a trust shortly thereafter."],"worked":["Original fictional $20,000 zero-rate assistance note; assume 10% of ORIGINAL principal forgiven per qualifying anniversary.","At year 0 remaining $20,000; after 3 anniversaries remaining $14,000; after 5 remaining $10,000.","At year 3 modeled sale: property $280,000, first mortgage payoff $225,000, subordinate payoff $14,000.","Proceeds before sale costs $41,000; hypothetical selling expenses 6%×$280,000=$16,800.","Modeled sale proceeds after liens and expenses $24,200; do not equate with taxable gain."],"practice":[["What happens if the buyer tries to refinance a first mortgage in year three?","Review the subordinate loan's recorded documents and current program requirements; a nonforgiven balance may become payable or prevent subordination."],["What is the difference between annual forgiveness and declining interest?","Forgiveness reduces legally owed principal if conditions are met; a 0%-rate loan accrues no ordinary interest under that assumption but may still have outstanding principal."]]},{"title":"Refinance decisions need a complete lien and cash-flow model","body":["Refinancing typically involves replacing the current first mortgage with a new loan. Some borrowers seek a lower interest rate, others seek a lower required payment, a different term, cash-out proceeds, or removal of mortgage-insurance costs. The goal must be stated because a smaller payment can result from a longer term rather than a cheaper loan. A refinance proposal should record old balance, remaining months, new rate, new term, paid or financed fees, and any second lien that must be discharged.","Suppose a household owes $210,000 on a first mortgage and $14,000 on an unforgiven assistance lien. A proposed refinance lender may require resolution of the subordinate lien. If $14,000 must be paid at refinancing, the new borrowing or cash requirement can be materially higher than the first-mortgage balance alone. The first loan's due-on-sale protections and the second loan's refinance terms address different legal questions; neither automatically cancels the other.","For a simplified interest-rate scenario, compare a $210,000 balance at an existing 6.75% note rate with twenty-six years remaining to a new $210,000 loan at 6.00% over thirty years with $5,000 fees. The payment calculations must reflect those terms and the old 312-month amortization. A naive calculation of monthly difference and fee payback is useful only as a first screen, because the new loan will end four years later if payments are not accelerated.","A rigorous analysis discounts the differences in future payments over the anticipated holding period, includes fee timing and payoff balances at exit, and tests rates, sale dates and the effect of additional liens. Confirm whether a mortgage is assumable, whether a subordinate loan may be paid off without penalty, and whether lender pricing changes with cash-out or combined debt. If the borrower must use emergency reserves to close the refinance, liquidity risk may outweigh projected monthly savings."],"worked":["Illustrative current first mortgage balance $210,000 and 26 years remaining at 6.75% versus a new 30-year 6.00% loan.","Current scheduled P&I calculated over remaining 312 months; new scheduled P&I over 360 months.","Add $5,000 new transaction fees paid in cash, plus any actual payoff required for the $14,000 subordinate lien.","For a fixed future sale date, compare all monthly installments plus each unpaid first/second-lien balance and upfront amounts.","Recalculate with a five-year rather than full-term horizon to avoid declaring a false saving from extending maturity."],"practice":[["Why can a refinanced loan have a lower payment yet higher total interest?","The rate can fall while repayment is stretched over more months; accumulated interest and fees must be modeled."],["Does the fact that Time To Own has zero required monthly payment imply it cannot affect a refinance?","No. The subordinate lien's payoff/subordination and transfer rules may affect lender underwriting and available proceeds."]]},{"title":"Moving a mortgaged home into a trust: the legal layers","body":["A trust is a legal arrangement regarding ownership and management of property, not a mechanism for instantly deleting a secured debt. The homeowner may use a revocable living trust to organize management, successor trusteeship and eventual property disposition, depending on state law and actual documents. The property's deed and recording determine how title is transferred; the signed note still defines personal repayment and the mortgage secures repayment unless a legally valid release occurs.","Federal law under 12 U.S.C. §1701j-3(d)(8) limits a lender's exercise of a due-on-sale clause for certain transfers into inter vivos trusts where the borrower remains a beneficiary and the transaction does not transfer occupancy rights. That protection is conditional and does not exempt every trust or every transaction. It does not mean a lender loses its lien. Other rules for notice, insurance, property taxes, subordinate programs and title companies may still matter.","Consider a family that owns a $250,000 property subject to a $237,500 mortgage and then executes a permitted qualifying trust transfer. Before and after, the economic home equity is approximately $12,500 under unchanged valuation and debt assumptions. Nothing about moving legal title automatically creates another $250,000 asset, eliminates mortgage interest, or transfers the value out of creditor reach. A trust may change administration and succession mechanics while leaving the household's economic exposure largely unchanged.","A learner should request the mortgage note and security instrument, property deed, trust terms, current title insurance instructions, beneficiary designations, and any assistance-lien conditions. Legal counsel should confirm title and deed requirements and whether the contemplated transfer triggers repayment, tax reporting or eligibility problems. The correct professional boundary is legal coordination, not presenting one-size-fits-all property-protection claims."],"worked":["Property $250,000; first mortgage $237,500; unchanged value and loan balance.","Before lawful trust deed transfer, modeled equity $12,500.","After lawful trust deed transfer with no debt discharge or sale, modeled economic equity still $12,500.","The mortgage and any separate assistance lien remain legally relevant; ownership labels do not repay debt.","The student's memo must distinguish federal first-loan protections from additional contract obligations."],"practice":[["Does the word 'irrevocable' in a trust automatically erase a mortgage lien?","No. Prior secured interests remain unless released or otherwise resolved under law and loan documents; the trust label is not a payoff."],["What is the statutory condition relevant to certain inter vivos trust transfers?","Borrower remains a beneficiary and transfer does not relate to a transfer of occupancy rights, among the statutory conditions; actual contracts and facts require legal review."]]},{"title":"Inheritance, beneficiary coordination and the future homeowner","body":["Owning real estate and directing what happens after death involve title, marital property rights, liens, probate or trust administration, insurance benefits and beneficiaries. A will, beneficiary designation and trust may govern different assets; one does not automatically supersede every other. Any successor receiving a home takes it subject to claims and ongoing costs unless those liabilities are lawfully discharged. Inheritance is valuable only if the beneficiary can support carrying costs or manage an appropriate sale.","A survivor may face property taxes, hazard insurance, mortgage payments, repairs and estate administration while the titled borrower is unavailable. A trust can help determine successor authority in particular structures, but the property may still be subject to secured debt. Life insurance paid to a beneficiary could potentially fund mortgage costs, but coverage limits, policy exclusions, ownership and beneficiary designations must be examined rather than assumed.","Prepare two cases: in one, heirs retain the residence and need enough reliable income to pay all housing costs; in the other, they sell at a lower property value and pay outstanding first and subordinate liens plus selling costs. These scenarios should incorporate local tax and home condition changes and should not rely on speculative appreciation. Financial planners coordinate with estate attorneys and tax advisers because capital-gains basis, estate exemptions and title rules may be fact-specific and change over time.","Program restrictions make this more delicate when assistance financing remains outstanding. A borrower hoping to deed the home into a trust for a daughter should determine whether this is an allowed transfer under every relevant document and whether the family could support expenses on one income. The student should produce a responsibilities map: title holder, borrower on note, mortgagee/lienholder, trustee, insurer, beneficiary, occupant, and tax-paying party."],"worked":["Hypothetical $250,000 home, $225,000 remaining first mortgage, $10,000 assistance lien, 6% selling costs.","Selling costs = $15,000; sale proceeds after loan payoffs and modeled expense = $0.","If home loses 10% to $225,000, selling cost at 6% is $13,500 and proceeds = −$23,500, before other estate costs.","If the family instead retains the home, model monthly mortgage + escrow + repair reserve + utilities + any subordinate installment.","A beneficiary receiving legal title cannot treat nominal gross home value as immediate usable inheritance."],"practice":[["Why is nominal home value not the same as a child's inheritance?","Outstanding liens, selling costs, estate expenses, ownership and tax rules, and the beneficiary's ability to carry costs determine the net outcome."],["What parties should review an assistance-financed trust transfer?","A qualified Connecticut attorney, the first-mortgage servicer/lender, CHFA/assistance servicer as applicable, title insurer and appropriate tax professional."]]},{"title":"Final applied scenario: a Connecticut family purchase with competing constraints","body":["The final case integrates mortgage mathematics, funding, tax, contractual assistance and family estate goals. Consider a household evaluating a $250,000 property with an assumed 6.50% first mortgage of $237,500, $7,500 in modeled closing/prepaid costs, a $20,000 possible assistance loan and only $11,000 of current liquid funds. The mortgage alone has approximately $1,501.16 monthly P&I. Add verified property-tax estimate, homeowners insurance, potential mortgage insurance, transportation, income variability and emergency repairs before asserting affordability.","The household may be eligible for a CHFA program, but 'possibly eligible' is not cash. The underwriting must verify the current offer, timing, borrower and property eligibility, and contract conditions. If the second lien is used to fund required closing money, its current legal balance, forgiven amounts and triggers must appear on later sale or refinance comparisons. Distinguish source-of-funds timing from gross cost.","Assume a hypothetical property tax bill of $5,831 annually and homeowners insurance of $1,800 annually. The simplified PITI estimate is $1,501.16+$485.92+$150=$2,137.08 per month, before mortgage insurance, maintenance, utilities and any subordinate payment. If net household take-home is $4,400 and essential nonhousing costs total $2,100, only about $162.92 remains after this limited PITI and nonhousing cost schedule. A $3,000 unexpected repair would exhaust more than eighteen months of the modeled margin.","The student must recommend between buying, delaying, negotiating, reducing purchase price or selecting another property. A thoughtful memo must identify legal and financial uncertainties rather than present an aggressive loan-to-value strategy as universally wise. Use actual CHFA and municipal information before any real application; treat the above figures as an instructional exercise."],"worked":["Purchase $250,000; first financing $237,500 at 6.50% 30 years ⇒ monthly P&I ≈$1,501.16.","Hypothetical tax $5,831/year=$485.92/month; hazard $1,800/year=$150/month.","Limited PITI ≈$2,137.08, before possible mortgage insurance, maintenance and utilities.","Net income $4,400−nonhousing essentials $2,100−limited PITI $2,137.08 ⇒ provisional monthly cash margin $162.92.","$3,000 repair divided by $162.92 margin ≈18.4 months to replenish in this narrow budget, and the true margin may be lower."],"practice":[["Write a 400-word recommendation using both a financial model and an official source.","Explain contractual assistance, remaining mortgage and liens, property-tax source date, household budget and at least one adverse scenario; identify unknowns."],["What must be checked before transferring title into a trust?","Legal deed/title, first and second lien terms, federal transfer protections, insurance coverage, assistance restrictions and state counsel advice."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CHFA · Time To Own (2026 public summary)","https://www.chfa.org/homebuyers-homeowners/homebuyers/time-to-own-down-payment-assistance-program-loan/"],["CHFA · Time To Own borrower notice (confirm current contract)","https://www.chfa.org/assets/1/6/028_Time_to_Own_%28TTO%29-_Applicant_Notice__TTODiscl_Rev._6-7-20221.pdf"],["CHFA · DAP second mortgage","https://www.chfa.org/homebuyers-homeowners/homebuyers/downpayment-assistance-program-dap-loan/"],["U.S. Code §1701j-3 · certain trust transfers","https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2024-title12/html/USCODE-2024-title12-chap13-sec1701j-3.htm"],["CFPB · Refinance comparison and mortgage shopping","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/mortgages/shopping-for-a-mortgage/"]],"assessment":{"title":"CHFA–mortgage–trust decision memorandum","prompt":"For a $250,000 hypothetical Connecticut home, produce the complete sources-and-uses schedule, 30-year first-mortgage payment, estimated PITI and liquidity stress test. Then build a conditional 10-year $20,000 assistance-loan forgiveness schedule and an early-sale payoff scenario. Compare refinancing in year three with and without a second-lien payoff, and write a trust-transfer risk checklist for a potential inheritance plan.","rubric":["First mortgage and monthly cash model (20%)","Aid eligibility versus conditional loan obligations (20%)","Year-by-year forgiveness and sale calculations (20%)","Refinance comparison and separate lien triggers (20%)","Legally cautious inheritance/trust and citations (20%)"]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Comprehensive Connecticut mortgage and inheritance decision","intro":"Independent transfer case: combine household underwriting, cash-to-close, property-tax risk, assistance liens, a refinance and a potential trust transfer. All amounts are fictional and no eligibility is assumed.","scenario":["Connecticut home price $250,000; down payment $12,500; 30-year first mortgage $237,500 at hypothetical 6.50%; $7,500 modeled nonrecoverable closing costs.","Opening cash $30,000, gross monthly income $6,000, net take-home $4,550, car and other debt payments $450, nonhousing essentials $1,950, maintenance reserve $300.","Property-tax placeholder $5,831 annually based on a fictional tax rate and assessed value. Annual homeowner insurance $1,800. Mortgage insurance, utilities, moving and other costs remain unquoted and must be researched.","A borrower asks about a possible $20,000 CHFA Time To Own subordinate loan; qualification, current availability, allowed uses and transfer limitations are unverified.","Year-three downside: sale price $230,000; 6% assumed sale charges; first loan amortized for 36 payments; assume exactly three earned annual forgiveness events on $20,000 assistance and the corresponding $14,000 unpaid subordinate balance."],"tasks":["Create the no-assistance closing balance sheet: record house, remaining cash, first lien, net worth. Explain the effect of down payment versus nonrecoverable charges.","Calculate level P&I from the note rate, plus simplified property-tax and hazard escrow, and estimate the household cash margin using all provided obligations. State missing bills.","Calculate hypothetical year-three first-loan remaining balance and sale proceeds after 6% costs and a $14,000 subordinate payoff; interpret negative proceeds.","Prepare an alternative in which the purchase is delayed and reserves are built. Demonstrate what exact monthly cash adjustment would make the plan sustainable.","Cite primary CFPB documents, CHFA current program pages, Connecticut tax data and the federal trust-transfer statute. State why no program eligibility or legal consequence is guaranteed."],"answer":["Without aid opening cash $30,000 minus $12,500 down and $7,500 nonrecoverable costs = $10,000; property $250,000 plus cash $10,000 minus first mortgage $237,500 = $22,500 modeled net worth.","First mortgage P&I approximately $1,501.16; property tax $485.92/month plus hazard $150/month gives limited PITI around $2,137.08.","Cash margin before mortgage insurance, utilities or other unmodeled spending: $4,550−$2,137.08−$450−$1,950−$300=−$287.08/month. Under these inputs it fails a sustainable cash-flow test before adding other costs.","After 36 installments at the assumed rate first-loan balance approximately $228,990.95; 6% selling costs on $230,000 are $13,800. With $14,000 unpaid subordinate claim, net proceeds $230,000−$13,800−$228,990.95−$14,000≈−$26,790.95.","A conditional offer of assistance is not an underwritten approval; the learner should compare delay, cheaper property, valid concessions, higher sustainable income and confirmed lower expenses. A trust transfer requires legal and first/second-lien review."],"rubric":["Financial accounting and source-of-funds reconciliation (20%)","Mortgage mathematics and correct remaining balance (20%)","Monthly affordability and shock analysis (20%)","CHFA and trust legal limitations (20%)","Independently sourced written recommendation (20%)"]}};
